---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { t } from "../../i18n/ui";
import addressSvg from "../../assets/contact/address-en.svg?raw";
import emailSvg from "../../assets/contact/email.svg?raw";

const lang = "en";
---

<Layout
    title={t(lang, "nav.datenschutz")}
    lang="en"
    description="Privacy Policy of Jannik Menzel: Information about the handling of personal data on this website."
    keywords="Privacy, Privacy Policy, Jannik Menzel, Data, GDPR, Website"
    og={{
        title: `${t(lang, "nav.datenschutz")} – Jannik Menzel`,
        description:
            "Privacy Policy of Jannik Menzel: Information about the handling of personal data on this website.",
        url: "https://www.jannikmenzel.me/en/datenschutz/",
        image: "https://www.jannikmenzel.me/social-preview.png",
    }}
    twitter={{
        title: "Privacy Policy – Jannik Menzel",
        description: "Privacy policy of the website of Jannik Menzel.",
        image: "https://www.jannikmenzel.me/social-preview.png",
    }}
    canonical="https://www.jannikmenzel.me/en/datenschutz/"
    structuredData={{
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "WebPage",
        name: t(lang, "nav.datenschutz"),
        description:
            "Privacy Policy of Jannik Menzel: Information about the handling of personal data on this website.",
        url: "https://www.jannikmenzel.me/en/datenschutz/",
    }}
>
    <main class="w-full container px-4 sm:px-16 mb-22 max-w-[1100px]">
        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="pt-20">
            <h1 class="text-center text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold font-display mb-4">
                {t(lang, "nav.datenschutz")}
            </h1>
            <div class="h-1 w-24 bg-primary mx-auto"></div>
        </header>

        <!-- Content -->
        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6 pt-22">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2 flex justify-between items-baseline">
                <span>Introduction</span>
                <span class="text-base font-thin">Status: November 14, 2026</span>
            </h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                This privacy policy applies to this website and my blog at blog.jannikmenzel.me.
                Projects within my freelance work are covered by a supplementary privacy policy that
                I provide to clients.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Who is responsible?</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                I am the controller responsible for data processing within the meaning of the GDPR:
            </p>

            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                Jannik Menzel<br />
                <span class="block [&>svg]:block" set:html={addressSvg} />
                Email: <span class="inline-block align-top [&>svg]:block" set:html={emailSvg} />
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Hosting (GitHub Pages)</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                This website is hosted via <strong>GitHub Pages</strong> (GitHub Inc., 88 Colin P. Kelly
                Jr. St., San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). Each time the website is accessed, GitHub automatically
                collects data in server log files, e.g., IP address, time, referrer URL, browser and requested
                page. I have no access to these log data myself. GitHub stores them only as long as necessary
                for delivery and security. The legal basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR. My legitimate
                interest lies in the reliable and secure provision of the website. This involves transferring
                data to the USA. GitHub is certified under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, so an adequacy
                decision pursuant to Art. 45 GDPR applies to the transfer. For more information, see
                <a
                    href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="underline">GitHub's Privacy Policy</a
                >.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Cloudflare</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                <strong>Cloudflare</strong> (Cloudflare, Inc., 101 Townsend St., San Francisco, CA 94107,
                USA) manages my domain (DNS) and acts as a reverse proxy and content delivery network
                between your browser and GitHub Pages. All requests to this website are therefore routed
                through Cloudflare. In doing so, Cloudflare processes in particular your IP address, date
                and time, the requested URL, the referrer, and browser and system information, and deletes
                it in accordance with the periods stated in its privacy policy. I use Cloudflare to deliver
                the website quickly, encrypted and protected against attacks (e.g., DDoS). Via "Network
                Error Logging", your browser may send technical error reports, e.g., on failed connections,
                to Cloudflare. To fend off automated access, Cloudflare may set technically necessary
                cookies (e.g.,
                <code>__cf_bm</code> or <code>cf_clearance</code>), which do not require consent
                pursuant to Section 25 (2) No. 2 TDDDG.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                The legal basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR. My legitimate interest lies in the
                secure and stable provision of the website. Cloudflare's terms of service include a
                data processing agreement (Data Processing Addendum) pursuant to Art. 28 GDPR.
                Processing may also take place in the USA. Cloudflare is certified under the EU-US
                Data Privacy Framework, so an adequacy decision pursuant to Art. 45 GDPR applies to
                the transfer. For more information, see
                <a
                    href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="underline">Cloudflare's Privacy Policy</a
                >.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="server" class="mb-6 sm:mb-6 scroll-mt-24">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">
                Own Servers and Protection Against Attacks
            </h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                The contact form, web analytics and blog travel via HTTPS through a server I rent
                from Hetzner Online GmbH (Industriestr. 25, 91710 Gunzenhausen, Germany) in its
                Falkenstein data center and from there, encrypted, to my own server in Germany. A
                data processing agreement pursuant to Art. 28 GDPR is in place with Hetzner. Both
                servers log connection data (IP address, time, requested address, browser
                identifier). I use it only for security, troubleshooting and abuse prevention and
                delete it automatically after 14 days at the latest.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                On the Hetzner server, the security software <strong>CrowdSec</strong> analyzes this connection
                data, blocks IP addresses with typical attack patterns and checks requests against a shared
                blocklist of known attackers. When an address is blocked, CrowdSec transmits the IP address,
                the detected type of attack and the time to CrowdSec SAS in France, which compiles this
                blocklist from it. The legal basis in each case is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR. My legitimate
                interest lies in protecting my servers and your data against attacks.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Storage in Your Browser</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                This website sets no cookies of its own. If you switch between light and dark design
                or between German and English, your browser stores this choice locally (<code
                    >theme</code
                >, <code>language</code>). These entries never leave your device and can be deleted
                at any time in your browser settings. The storage is required for the function you
                requested and does not require consent pursuant to Section 25 (2) No. 2 TDDDG.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">
                Web Analytics (Plausible Analytics)
            </h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                For audience measurement, I use <strong>Plausible Analytics</strong> (Community Edition)
                on my own server (see "Own Servers"). No other third parties receive any data. Plausible
                sets no cookies and stores no IP addresses. To count visitors, it creates a hash from
                the IP address and browser identifier using a key that changes daily, so you are not recognized
                the next day. Plausible determines the country locally. Only aggregated data such as page
                visited, referrer, browser, operating system, device type and country is stored.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                The legal basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR. My legitimate interest is to evaluate
                and improve my website in a data-minimizing way. You can prevent collection by
                disabling JavaScript or using a content blocker.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Contact Form</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                If you write to me via the contact form, I process your input (name, email address,
                subject, message) to handle your request and possible follow-up questions. Providing
                this data is voluntary, but without it I cannot handle your request. The legal basis
                is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. b GDPR insofar as your inquiry relates to placing an order or
                an existing contractual relationship, and otherwise Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR based
                on my legitimate interest in responding to your inquiry. I delete the data once your
                inquiry has been fully processed and no statutory retention obligations apply.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                I process the form with the automation software <strong>n8n</strong> on my own server
                (see "Own Servers"). n8n checks your input for completeness and spam and forwards it to
                my mailbox by email (see "Email Contact"). No other third parties receive any data. My
                server only keeps a request if forwarding fails, and then for no more than 14 days.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Email Contact</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                If you send me an email, I store the data it contains (e.g., name, email address,
                message) to handle your inquiry. The legal basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. b GDPR
                insofar as the inquiry serves contract initiation or performance, and otherwise Art.
                6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR based on my legitimate interest in responding to your inquiry.
                I delete the data once your inquiry has been fully processed and no statutory
                retention obligations apply.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                My emails are stored at mailbox.org, a service of
                <strong>Heinlein Hosting GmbH</strong> (Schwedter Straße 8/9A, 10119 Berlin, Germany),
                on servers in Germany. This also applies to inquiries forwarded from the contact form.
                The legal basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR. My legitimate interest lies in reliable
                email communication. A data processing agreement pursuant to Art. 28 GDPR is in place
                with the provider. For more information, see
                <a
                    href="https://mailbox.org/en/data-protection/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="underline">mailbox.org's data protection information</a
                >.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="blog" class="mb-6 sm:mb-6 scroll-mt-24">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Blog (blog.jannikmenzel.me)</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                I run my blog with the open-source software <strong>Ghost</strong> on my own server (see
                "Own Servers"). For the blog, Cloudflare only provides domain management (DNS). Requests
                are routed neither through Cloudflare nor through GitHub. The blog loads fonts, scripts
                and images exclusively from my server. There is no web analytics on the blog.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                <strong>Account:</strong> If you register on the blog, I store your email address, your
                name, the time of registration and of your last visit. Providing this data is voluntary,
                but an account is not possible without it. There is no password. To sign in, you receive
                a link by email. I send these emails and a welcome email via mailbox.org (see "Email Contact").
                The legal basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. b GDPR. Your data remains stored until you have
                your account deleted. An email to me is enough for this.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                <strong>Comments:</strong> If you write a comment, your name and your comment are displayed
                publicly below the article. Comments remain stored until you or I delete them. The legal
                basis is Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. b GDPR.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                <strong>Cookies:</strong> Without signing in, the blog sets no cookies. After you sign
                in, the cookies <code>ghost-members-ssr</code> and
                <code>ghost-members-ssr.sig</code> store your session. Your browser stores your choice
                between the light and dark design locally (<code>jm-theme</code>). Both are
                technically necessary and do not require consent pursuant to Section 25 (2) No. 2
                TDDDG.
            </p>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify mt-4 [hyphens:auto]">
                <strong>Backups:</strong> The blog's data is included in the backups on my server. An
                encrypted copy is also stored on my server at Hetzner Online GmbH. Deleted data disappears
                from the backups after seven months at the latest.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">Your Rights</h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                You have the right of access (Art. 15 GDPR), rectification (Art. 16), erasure (Art.
                17), restriction of processing (Art. 18), data portability (Art. 20) and objection
                (Art. 21). An informal email to me is enough. You can also lodge a complaint with a
                data protection supervisory authority pursuant to Art. 77 GDPR, in particular in the
                member state of your habitual residence, place of work or place of the alleged
                infringement.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-6 sm:mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-medium mb-2">
                RIGHT TO OBJECT PURSUANT TO ART. 21 GDPR
            </h2>
            <p class="font-thin text-justify [hyphens:auto]">
                You have the right, on grounds relating to your particular situation, to object at
                any time to the processing of personal data concerning you that is carried out on
                the basis of Art. 6 Para. 1 lit. f GDPR (data processing based on a balancing of
                interests). If you object, I will no longer process your personal data unless I can
                demonstrate compelling legitimate grounds for the processing that override your
                interests, rights and freedoms, or the processing serves the establishment, exercise
                or defense of legal claims.
            </p>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
